John H. wrote:
> tg wrote:
> 
>>What do you generally do - make a wish right away [...]
> 
> If your character has yet to acquire DSM from another source, it's
> really not a hard choice to make [...]  The AC bonus is too great
> to ignore, plus the opportunity to get a major intrinsic out of the way
> to boot [...].
> 
> Sometimes I wonder what Nethack would be like without DSM, or at least
> with it knocked down a few AC points so it wasn't the absolute best
> armor in the game.

Well, I know what you mean, but keep in mind that a) you have to kill a
_dragon_ to obtain it, which is not trivial for early/mid characters, b)
the dragon has to leave scales, c) you get it "quite late" in the game,
so you usually need other armour in the beginning, that you may already
have enchanted to an AC value that is equivalent to the basic dragon AC
(+4 elven mithril equals +0 dragon mail), and d) you still get only one
extrinsic resistance or property, and you may not choose which one. (The
point that a wand of wishing makes everything possible, in early or mid
game, is a "problem" with the wand not with the dragon mail.) I see the
dragon mail as a milestone, comparable to the point when I got a unicorn
horn, or when I've successfully done a quest; it indeed raises you to a
higher (survival-)level, but that's the game (I think), and I think it's
fine that way.

>  I wonder if the game would be improved as a result.
>  Sometimes I even suspect it would.  Ah well.

In which respect to you think it might be an improvement?

Fewer points of AC that you mentioned above wouldn't really change things
in the light of the armour tank the character usually is at that stage.
Where these points do matter is visible in the context of this thread; if
you get them early (and without the hard work to kill a dragon yourself),
if you get a wish. But, again, that's more a problem with the wishes[*]
made available early than with the specific type of mail.

Janis

[*] Not that I think early wishes would be that big a problem, after all
you have to decide what to wish for and a single wish won't solve all of
your survival problems; you don't know what you'll need 50 turns later.
Therefore the suggestion to delay wishes as far as safely possible. (More
of a problem are massive wishes as provided by an early wand of wishing.)
A single early wish for DSM still increases your survival chance, but
that's okay; hey, it's a _wish_ after all!


